, "lead_after": 0.0, "points_lost": -0.12, "best": "J7"}], "review": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "title": "Two 20-Point Swings and a Draw", "summary": "Opus 5.5 and the 12k finished level, a draw. Opus 5.5 fell behind early, with Black's lead at -20.7 after move 13 and still -20.3 after move 25. Then the game turned on two matching errors: Opus 5.5's J3 on move 29 lost 20.3 points, and the 12k's reply J5 on move 30 lost the same 20.3 and put the lead back at -0.1. Both sides averaged 3.7 points lost per move, and the endgame stayed at about 0.0 until the two passes.", "moments": [{"turn": 13, "text": "Opus 5.5 played H3 and lost 11.4 points, taking Black's lead from -9.4 to -20.7. The engine preferred G5. Black's stones at H4, H3, G3 and G2 stayed low on the right edge while White held E5 and H5 in the centre."}, {"turn": 24, "text": "The 12k's D4 lost 13.5 points, moving Black's lead from -20.1 to -6.6. The engine's choice was H1. It was the first sign that the 12k could give a big lead back."}, {"turn": 29, "text": "Black's J3 is the worst move of Opus 5.5's game, losing 20.3 points as the lead went from -0.1 to -20.4. Before it the game was level, and the engine wanted E1 instead."}, {"turn": 30, "text": "The 12k answered J5 and lost exactly as much, 20.3 points, with the lead going from -20.4 back to -0.1. The engine's choice was F1. A 20-point swing in each direction in two moves left the game level."}, {"turn": 31, "text": "Opus 5.5 played E1, the engine's own first choice, with the lead steady at -0.1 → -0.0. Black had been behind for most of the game and now kept the draw. The rest of the game stayed within 0.1 points of level, down to the final passes."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "after.py applies one named move (e.g. C4, D2, E1) to a pasted board, removes captures and prints group liberties, libs.py just prints group liberties, and count.py counts stones and territory on the current board; none of them loops over candidate mov